<h1>Details Dialog</h1>
<p>The execution tree shows what happened in numbers: planned, done, passed, failed. The <strong>Details</strong>
	dialog shows what happened in full. It opens on any row of the tree and gathers everything that belongs to that
	row's scope: the configuration of every item in it, with default, planned and run values side by side, and a
	results tree that goes down through cycles, cases, case cycles, flows and steps, with the status, timing and
	descriptions of each. Earlier pages opened it on single tests; this page opens it at the other levels and
	walks through its parts.</p>
<p>The session in the controller is a workspace. It holds every suite and test loaded, relevant to the question at
	hand or not, and a run of a whole suite mixes hundreds of results. The Details dialog is the tool that scopes
	that workspace to a chosen level and summarizes it there, which is why it exists at every level from the
	session row down to a single case, and why its exports are the way results leave the controller.</p>
<p>To have something to look at, run the five tests of the <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group with their
	folders set as on <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/orcraft-statuses/">Orcraft Statuses</a>: the group then
	holds a pass with warnings, a failure, an error, a partly failing checkpoint run and a passing run with result
	fields.</p>

<h2 id="the-session-level">The session level</h2>
<p>Select the <strong>Session</strong> row at the top of the tree and click <strong>Details</strong> in the action
	bar, or right-click the row and choose it. A double-click on any row of the tree opens the same dialog for that
	row, which is the quickest way in:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/details-dialog/details-session.png", "Session Details dialog with the configuration of every loaded suite, group and test nested on the left, a results tree of the whole session on the right with Not Run rows for tests that have not run, and an Items Filter with Pass, Warn, Fail, Error, Not Run and All chips", "large") }}
<p>The left half, <strong>Session Configurations</strong>, nests the configuration of every loaded item: the
	suite, each group in it, each test in the group with its <strong>Default Value</strong>, <strong>Plan
	Value</strong> and <strong>Run Value</strong> columns. The right half, <strong>Session Results</strong>, starts
	with the summary strip the tree also shows and then lists every item as a tree. Tests that have not run appear
	with <strong>Not Run</strong> rows for the cases they plan, so the scope is complete rather than limited to what
	has happened.</p>
<p>At the bottom, the <strong>Items Filter</strong> counts the rows of the results tree by status:
	<strong>Pass</strong>, <strong>Warn</strong>, <strong>Fail</strong>, <strong>Error</strong>,
	<strong>Not Run</strong> and <strong>All</strong>. The counts are rows, so a case and its case cycle count
	twice; they are there to say how much of each kind the tree holds and to narrow it.</p>

<h2 id="the-group-level">The group level</h2>
<p>Close the dialog, select the <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group and open Details again. Then click
	the <strong>Fail</strong> chip:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/details-dialog/details-group-fail-filter.png", "Details dialog of the Statuses and Results group with the Fail chip active, the results tree narrowed to the failing cases of the Status: Fail and Checkpoints runs, and the group's configuration and case tables on the left", "large") }}
<p>The dialog is now scoped to the group: its description and its tests' configurations on the left, and only the
	group's results on the right. With <strong>Fail</strong> active the tree keeps the rows that failed and the
	rows above them, so the failing cases of <strong>Status: Fail</strong> and <strong>Checkpoints</strong> are
	listed together with the test runs they belong to and nothing else. Click <strong>All</strong> to restore the
	tree. The chips work the same way as the status chips above the execution tree, one level deeper.</p>

<h2 id="the-test-level">The test level</h2>
<p>Open Details on <strong>Status: Fail</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/details-dialog/details-test.png", "Details dialog of the Status: Fail test with its description, its configuration table, the List Dimensions and case tables, and a results tree of sixteen cases with two passing and fourteen failing", "large") }}
<p>A test's dialog adds what only a test has: the <strong>Test Description</strong> from the config class, the
	<strong>List Dimensions</strong> table with the default, planned and run values of each matrix dimension, and
	the case table with a <strong>Planned</strong> and a <strong>Run</strong> check per case. The results tree runs
	from the test run through its cycle to every case and case cycle, and further down to flows and steps when the
	test has them. Scrolling the tree to the right reveals the columns every row carries: the endpoint, the status,
	the start and finish times and the elapsed time, the error, failure and warning descriptions, the repository,
	branch and commit of the test, and any <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/result-fields/">result
	fields</a> the test declares.</p>
<p>The buttons above the tree help with large scopes. <strong>Refresh</strong> re-reads the results while a run
	is still going, the <strong>&minus;</strong> and <strong>+</strong> buttons collapse and expand every row, and
	the tree's own arrows fold single branches.</p>

<h2 id="the-test-run-level">The test run level</h2>
<p>A test keeps every run it has made as a <strong>Test Run</strong> row beneath it, the older ones greyed as
	superseded. The test level gathers all of them; a run row scopes the dialog to one. Run
	<strong>Status: Fail</strong> a second time, select the newer run row and open Details on it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/details-dialog/two-runs-tree.png", "Execution tree with two Test Run rows under the Status: Fail test, the older one greyed and the newer one selected, both reading Fail with 2 passed and 14 failed cases", "large") }}
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/details-dialog/details-run.png", "Details dialog titled Test Run: Status: Fail: Details, with the test's description, its configuration with the run values, the dimension and case tables, and a results tree of a single Test Run row with its cycle and sixteen cases", "large") }}
<p>The title says <strong>Test Run</strong>, and the results tree starts at that one run rather than at the test,
	so its counts and its Items Filter cover this run alone. The <strong>Run Value</strong> column on the left
	holds what this run actually used, which matters when the plan has changed since: a run keeps its own
	configuration, and its dialog shows that one. The run row also carries a <strong>Planned</strong> column
	saying whether the run was made with the planned configuration or with values edited on the run itself. This
	is the level to open, and to export, when one run is to be reported or handed to an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/results/">AI agent</a> for a look at a failure.</p>

<h2 id="exporting-the-scope">Exporting the scope</h2>
<p>The two buttons at the bottom left take the dialog's scope out of the controller.</p>
<h3 id="export-to-xlsx">Export to XLSX</h3>
<p><strong>Export to XLSX</strong> writes a workbook with the summary, the configurations and the results tree of
	the scope, every column included. It is the format for a report or for analysis in a spreadsheet.</p>
<h3 id="export-for-agents">Export for Agents</h3>
<p><strong>Export for Agents</strong> writes a folder with a <code>summary.txt</code> of the scope and a
	<code>logs</code> folder holding the run logs of the scope with their colour codes stripped. It is the format
	for handing results to an <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/results/">AI agent</a>, which reads plain text
	better than a workbook and needs the logs next to the summary to explain a failure.</p>
<p>Both exports contain exactly what the dialog shows, at the level it was opened on. Opening it on the session
	exports everything; opening it on one failing test exports that test.</p>

<p>The dialog gathers the logs it exports from the same place the controller shows them. The next page,
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/log-frame/">Log Frame</a>, is about that place.</p>
